instrument check: clustered beats hash-assigned by 30.7% (0.9935 -> 0.6887)

VERDICT: correctness PASSES: zero aliasing, 336x smaller. RATE DEFERRED -- the error numbers rest on a synthetic spectrum (0.7) and an ungrounded threshold (0.25); re-run against the real table once the trunk is trained. The ordering is what transfers: hash-assigned 0.994 vs clustered 0.689, and frequency-weighted 0.297 with tiering

WHAT IS SETTLED AND WHAT IS NOT:
  settled  - GNSC's assignment aliases 128k tokens onto K vectors, and a
             full splitmix64 on the high bits fixes it to zero. Exact.
  settled  - the memory arithmetic, and that hash-assigned PQ (the only
             variant needing no pre-trained table) reconstructs NOTHING:
             ~0.99 relative error however much it compresses.
  settled  - the ordering: clustering beats hashing, smaller sub_dim beats
             larger, and frequency tiering beats both per MB.
  NOT      - whether any of it costs the model anything. That needs a
             trained table, and the honest answer today is 'unmeasured'.

  So the shape of the recommendation is already clear: the from-scratch
  variant is the one that does not work, and clustered PQ + a static
  frequency router is a POST-training compression step on a finished base.
  That fits no-retraining exactly -- compress the base once, ship it.